-- title:
--   A Diaz candidate is carried onto an ordinary transcendental point of the same circle
-- statement:
--   Let $u \in \mathbb{C}$ be a **candidate** for Diaz's modulus conjecture — $u \neq 0$ with $e^{u}$ algebraic over $\mathbb{Q}$ — and let $r \in \bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ be non-zero and real ($\bar r = r$) with
--
--   $$u\,\bar u  =  r^{2},$$
--
--   so that $r = |u|$ is algebraic. Then there exist a complex number $t$ and a ring homomorphism $\Phi : \mathbb{C} \to \mathbb{C}$ such that
--
--   $$t \neq 0, \qquad t \ \text{transcendental over } \bar{\mathbb{Q}}, \qquad \Phi|_{\bar{\mathbb{Q}}} = \mathrm{id}, \qquad \Phi(u) = t, \qquad \forall\, z \in \bar{\mathbb{Q}}(u),\ \Phi(\bar z) = \overline{\Phi(z)} .$$
--
--   Here $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}(u)$ is the subfield of $\mathbb{C}$ generated by the algebraic numbers together with $u$.
--
--   **Why.** Three ingredients. The circle $z\bar z = r^{2}$ carries a point $t$ transcendental over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`Diaz.exists_transcendental_on_circle`, whose witness is $r e^{i}$; realness of $r$ is what converts $t\bar t = r\bar r$ into $t\bar t = r^{2}$). A candidate is itself transcendental over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$, by Hermite–Lindemann. And two transcendental points of one circle over a conjugation-stable base are carried onto each other by a ring endomorphism of $\mathbb{C}$ that fixes the base and intertwines conjugation (`Diaz.exists_conj_intertwining`). The intertwining is not arranged but forced, because $\bar u = r^{2}/u$ makes conjugation a rational function of the generator.
--
--   **What is deliberately not claimed.** It is *not* asserted that $e^{t}$ is transcendental. Were it algebraic, $t$ would itself be a candidate, so such an assertion would be an instance of Diaz's conjecture rather than something proved here.
--
--   **Role — this is the headline result of the development.** It says that a hypothetical counterexample to Diaz's modulus conjecture and an ordinary point of the same circle are related by an embedding of $\mathbb{C}$ into itself fixing every algebraic number, matching the two points, and respecting complex conjugation on the field the candidate generates. Consequently no invariant built from that field's structure together with its involution — no rank, no structural rank over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$, no vanishing matrix coefficient — can separate the two. Since the ordinary point is not a counterexample, no such invariant can certify that the candidate is one: a whole class of algebraic proof strategies provably cannot settle the conjecture. The one thing the ordinary point lacks is that its exponential be algebraic, and that is exactly the input no algebraic argument reaches. The statement runs from the arithmetic hypotheses to the endomorphism with no step left in prose.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/carlok/diaz-modulus-lean/blob/801802b8ac052dff50baf17ac4a7ceac3e994ca9/Diaz/Instantiation.lean#L77-L106

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_Diaz_Closure
import Definitions.Def_Diaz_Instantiation

open ComplexConjugate
open Diaz

theorem Diaz.candidate_indistinguishable
    {u r : ℂ} (hu0 : u ≠ 0) (hexp : IsAlgebraic ℚ (Complex.exp u))
    (hr : r ∈ Qbar) (hrr : conj r = r) (hr0 : r ≠ 0)
    (h : u * conj u = r ^ 2) :
    ∃ t : ℂ, t ≠ 0 ∧ Transcendental (↥Qbar) t ∧
      ∃ Φ : ℂ →+* ℂ, (∀ a ∈ Qbar, Φ a = a) ∧ Φ u = t
        ∧ ∀ z ∈ hull Qbar u, Φ (conj z) = conj (Φ z) := by sorry
